File map. The evaluation logs name each oracle model by its build tag: _sig_hs is tools/silu_integer_oracle.py
--mode hardsigmoid, _sig_exact is --mode sigmoid, _sig_silu is --mode silu, _sig_pl3/_sig_pl4 are --mode pl
--lines 3/4. oracle_rebuild_sha256.log rebuilt all 13 from the committed tool: byte-identical to the evaluated
models. fp32_pl_fits.log did the same for the 8 FP32 form models (tools/silu_fp32_forms.py). The two keep-Sigmoid
quantizations ran from a scratch copy of tools/quantize_keep_sigmoid.py and were not re-run from tools/.

A first PL oracle clamped qh at 127, which the candidate core expression does not do (it reaches 128 when g
saturates at 64); its evaluations were discarded before any figure was recorded and are not in this directory.
The PL mode now asserts, for every (s1, s2) pair, that ORT's float path equals the integer core expression on all
256 inputs.

Not run: the candidate epilogue on the NPU (the kernel program is unchanged); the C++ expression executed off-device
against the Python mirror (no aie_api native build here); the full 5,000 images; AMD's stack on these oracle models
(they contain Gather tables, not a vendor graph). Inference latencies inside the eval logs were taken with other
evaluations running on the same CPU and are not measurements.
